+# Full readable study — S5-CLM-00003643
+
+## FS-N0 — semantic identity
+
+**Hypotheses.** The frozen record, source bytes, declaration span, provider revision, and Stage6 alias have the digests recorded in the intake and crosswalk. **Inference.** Normalize the two successive universal binders to an explicit natural-number binder and implication; preserve the meanings of `f`, exponentiation, subtraction, order, and `Nat.choose`. **Output.** A bidirectional transport between the frozen root surface and the claim-owned curried conjunction. **Formal anchor.** `L-STMT` and `L-AUDIT`. **Downstream use.** It fixes the proposition composed at N5. **Exceptional cases.** No numeric provider module is parsed as a canonical import, and no local symbol may shadow a source symbol. **Trust boundary.** The worker binds frozen bytes; the Master independently re-elaborates both sides and recomputes the semantic environment.
+
+## FS-N1 — extremal lower construction
+
+**Hypotheses.** Fix `n ≥ 3`. Use the classical separated-block construction in a generic affine chart. **Inference.** Starting from the one-point base at `n=2`, replace the configuration by two sufficiently separated, slightly perturbed affine copies. The separation invariant makes any convex subset meeting both blocks lose an extreme contribution in one block; induction bounds its size by `n-1`. Perturbations avoid all finitely many collinearity equations without changing the strict orientation signs. The cardinality doubles at each step, giving `2^(n-2)` points. **Output.** A general-position configuration of that size containing no convex `n`-subset; every smaller size inherits a counterexample by taking a subset. **Formal anchor.** lower geometric certificate consumed by `erdosSzekeresBounds_lower_projection`. **Downstream use.** N2 excludes every cardinality below the claimed lower threshold from `cardSet n`. **Exceptional cases.** At `n=3` the construction has two points; subsets of size below `n` cannot contain an `n`-subset. **Trust boundary.** Orientation/separation and perturbation are mathematical proof obligations, never oracle assumptions.
+
+## FS-N2 — lower transfer to the infimum
+
+**Hypotheses.** N1 supplies, for every `N ≤ 2^(n-2)`, an `N`-point general-position counterexample. The source definitions identify `cardSet n` with forcing cardinalities and `f n` with its natural-number infimum. **Inference.** A forcing cardinality cannot be at most `2^(n-2)`, since its universal property would apply to the corresponding counterexample. Thus every member of `cardSet n` is at least `2^(n-2)+1`; the same bound holds for its infimum. **Output.** `2^(n-2)+1 ≤ f n`. **Formal anchor.** lower projection in `Proof.lean`. **Downstream use.** Left input of N5. **Exceptional cases.** Nonemptiness of the forcing set is supplied independently by N3, so the natural infimum is not being used vacuously. **Trust boundary.** The order/infimum step is kernel checked against the pinned natural-number definitions.
+
+## FS-N3 — cup–cap upper theorem
+
+**Hypotheses.** Fix `n ≥ 3` and a finite planar set in general position. Choose a direction not orthogonal to any pair difference, so projected coordinates are distinct. **Inference.** Order points by projection. Classify each point by the largest cup and cap ending there. If there is no `r`-cup and no `s`-cap, the cup–cap induction partitions the possible endpoint classes with Pascal recurrence, giving at most `choose(r+s-4,r-2)` points. Therefore one additional point forces an `r`-cup or `s`-cap. Taking `r=s=n`, either monotone-turn chain has all its vertices on the boundary of its convex hull, hence is a convex-independent `n`-set. **Output.** Every general-position set of `choose(2*n-4,n-2)+1` points contains a convex `n`-gon. **Formal anchor.** upper geometric certificate consumed by `erdosSzekeresBounds_upper_projection`. **Downstream use.** N4 places the explicit cardinality in `cardSet n`. **Exceptional cases.** The generic projection handles equal original x-coordinates; no-three-collinear makes every turn strict; cup and cap cases are symmetric. **Trust boundary.** Pascal/Vandermonde arithmetic and the chain-to-convexity implication are explicit proof obligations.
+
+## FS-N4 — upper transfer to the infimum
+
+**Hypotheses.** N3 proves the universal forcing property at `N = choose(2*n-4,n-2)+1`; the input set is exactly the finite set quantified by `cardSet n`. **Inference.** Insert N into `cardSet n`, then apply the defining least-element property of `Nat.sInf` to this member. **Output.** `f n ≤ choose(2*n-4,n-2)+1`. **Formal anchor.** upper projection in `Proof.lean`. **Downstream use.** Right input of N5 and nonemptiness used in N2. **Exceptional cases.** Natural truncated subtraction is harmless because `n ≥ 3`; the chosen parameters are at least two. **Trust boundary.** Membership, coercions between finite sets and sets, and the infimum inequality are kernel checked.
+
+## FS-N5 — root composition
+
+**Hypotheses.** N0 fixes the exact semantic root; N2 and N4 establish its two inequalities for the same arbitrary `n ≥ 3`. **Inference.** Introduce `n` and its lower-bound hypothesis, then form the conjunction of the lower and upper certificates. **Output.** `∀ n ≥ 3, 2^(n-2)+1 ≤ f n ∧ f n ≤ choose(2*n-4,n-2)+1`. **Formal anchor.** `L-COMP`, with reverse audit at `L-AUDIT`. **Downstream use.** This is the release root and Stage6-alias payload. **Exceptional cases.** There is no assertion for `n<3`, no discarded branch, and neither inequality is inferred from the other. **Trust boundary.** Worker validation is semantic/evidence preflight only; canonical trust-zero compilation and acceptance belong to the Master.
